            {
              "@id": "urn:uuid:96380fd9-ae59-5f86-9bd3-86d054f38423",
              "@type": "uco-observable:ExtractedString",
              "uco-observable:stringValue": "Case 9:24-cr-80053-AMC Document 98 Entered on FLSD Docket 07/11/2025 Page 1 of 17\n\nUNITED STATES DISTRICT COURT\nSOUTHERN DISTRICT OF FLORIDA\n\nCASE NO. 24-80053-CR-CANNON\n\nUNITED STATES OF AMERICA\n\nvs.\n\nTHOMAS PETER KATSAMPES,\n\nDefendant.\n__________________________________/\n\nGOVERNMENT\u2019S SENTENCING MEMORANDUM AS TO\nDEFENDANT THOMAS PETER KATSAMPES\n\nCOMES NOW, the United States of America, by and through its undersigned attorneys,\n\nand respectfully submits this sentencing memorandum as to Thomas Peter Katsampes (the\n\n\u201cdefendant\u201d). 1\n\nCrimes involving the sexual abuse of children are incalculably serious. Offenders who\n\ntraffic in child pornography inflict grave, lifelong trauma on their victims. And far too often, these\n\nvictims go unidentified, meaning their suffering goes unvoiced. As one court put it,\n\n[c]hild pornography is a vile, heinous crime. Mention the term to your average\nAmerican and he responds with immediate disgust and a sense of unease. However,\nonce it enters the legal system, child pornography undergoes sterilization. The\nsterilization goes far beyond properly removing emotion from sentencing decisions.\nImages are described in the most clinical sense. Victims all too often remain\nnameless. The only emotions on display are those of defendants, sorry that their\nactions were discovered by law enforcement.\n\nUnited States v. Cunningham, 680 F. Supp. 2d 844, 847 (N.D. Ohio 2010). Here, the defendant\n\nhelped moderate a site where thousands of users trafficked in countless images and videos\n\ndepicting the sexual exploitation, abuse, and even torture of such victims. Given the exceptionally\n\n1\nFor the Court\u2019s awareness, the government intends to call a law enforcement witness to testify at the\nsentencing hearing on matters related to the defendant\u2019s objections to the PSR. [ECF No. 94]. The\ngovernment anticipates that this testimony should not take more than an hour.\nCase 9:24-cr-80053-AMC Document 98 Entered on FLSD Docket 07/11/2025 Page 2 of 17\n\nserious nature of this case and the sentences imposed on the defendant\u2019s co-conspirators, the\n\ngovernment respectfully recommends that the Court impose a sentence of 292 months, which is\n\nthe low end of the recommended sentencing range under the Guidelines. This sentence is fully\n\njustified by the sentencing factors in 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 3553(a), and it is consistent with the sentences\n\nthis Court has already handed down to the defendant\u2019s co-conspirators.\n\nBACKGROUND\n\nAs the Court well knows, \u201cWebsite A\u201d was an online sanctuary for pedophiles and child\n\npredators. For years, it was among the largest private meeting places on the dark web for users to\n\ndiscuss and share child pornography. The site operated as an online forum divided into chatrooms\n\nof varied exclusivity, where thousands of offenders from around the world could gather to explore\n\ntheir shared sexual interest in children, validate each other\u2019s conduct and destructive sexual\n\nimpulses, share tips on how to abuse children and avoid detection, and embolden each other to\n\nescalate their behavior. [See ECF No. 93 (\u201cPSR\u201d) at \u00b6\u00b6 6-9]. An initial, public-facing chatroom\n\nwas accessible to registered members and new visitors, where anyone could post, view, and discuss\n\nchild pornography. [Id. at \u00b6\u00b6 9-10]. Moderators like the defendant advised users in this room that\n\nregularly sharing child pornography would earn them an invitation into an interior chatroom,\n\nwhich was a more curated version of the public chatroom. [Id. at \u00b6\u00b6 9-10, 126(d)-(e)]. Beyond\n\nthese two rooms were various specialized chatrooms to which members gained access by posting\n\neven more child pornography. [Id. at \u00b6 12]. These included rooms dedicated to child pornography\n\ndepicting the sexual abuse of girls; the sexual abuse of infants and toddlers; and minors being\n\nsubjected to bestiality, sexual abuse involving \u201cscat\u201d or human excrement, and sexual violence\n\nand torture. [Id.]. These topics were not suggestions; rather, staff members and moderators ensured\n\nthat users only posted on-theme content in these rooms. [See, e.g., id. at \u00b6\u00b6 9-18]. Website A also\n\n2\nCase 9:24-cr-80053-AMC Document 98 Entered on FLSD Docket 07/11/2025 Page 3 of 17\n\ncontained a chatroom where users could share detailed advice on how to avoid detection by law\n\nenforcement, including through the use of sophisticated technologies. [Id. at \u00b6\u00b6 13, 17].\n\nSuch a large and active community, of course, can never exist in the real world. It can only\n\nexist online, and in the darkest corners of the internet. Nor could Website A have existed without\n\nthe defendant and others like him. While the defendant was not a founding member of the site, he\n\nwas nevertheless an important user who, after months of active participation, was promoted to a\n\nmoderating position by the site\u2019s leader. [PSR at \u00b6\u00b6 124, 126(b)]. 2 In fact, the defendant was\n\npersonally trained on how to moderate Website A\u2019s users by the site\u2019s leader, who gave the\n\ndefendant clear guidance on how to \u201ccheck in\u201d to the \u201cstaff room\u201d when he starts a moderating\n\nshift and how to \u201ccheck out\u201d when he ends his shift. The head administrator then explained to the\n\ndefendant that he (1) was responsible for \u201ccoaching\u201d lower ranking users on how to share child\n\npornography, (2) should be \u201ccourteous\u201d when muting lower-ranking users or correcting their posts,\n\nand (3) now outranked broad swaths of users on the site.\n\nThe defendant took his moderating role seriously. As a moderator, he, inter alia, instructed\n\nusers on how to share child pornography on the site and cautioned them that failure to post illicit\n\nmaterial in accordance with the site\u2019s rules could result in its removal. [Id. at \u00b6 124, 126(b)-(e)].\n\nHe likewise advised users on the site\u2019s other rules\u2014including to post only \u201con-topic\u201d content (that\n\nis, content depicting the sexual abuse of children) and to label such content based on what it\n\ndepicts\u2014and enticed users to share additional child pornography by offering them greater access\n\nto Website A in return. [Id.]\n\n2\nThe defendant received his first promotion on the site in March 2022, which he earned by sharing large\nquantities of child pornography. [PSR at \u00b6 126(a)]. As the site\u2019s rules explained, this promotion allowed\nhim greater access to more exclusive repositories of child pornography on the site, although he was not yet\na moderator. [Id.] Approximately eight months later, the site\u2019s leader selected the defendant for promotion\nto a moderating role. [Id. at \u00b6 126(b)].\n\n3\nCase 9:24-cr-80053-AMC Document 98 Entered on FLSD Docket 07/11/2025 Page 4 of 17\n\nIn addition to his moderating responsibilities, the defendant was also a frequent contributor\n\nto Website A\u2019s overall enterprise: the proliferation of material depicting the sexual abuse of\n\nchildren. For example, in September 2022, the defendant made a post with the text \u201cgirl gets her\n\npretty slit toyed\u201d and shared a link for other users on the site to a series of images depicting an\n\nadult man inserting phallic objects into the anus and vagina of a fully nude, prepubescent girl. [Id.\n\nat \u00b6 127(b)]. The defendant likewise solicited other users to engage in \u201croleplaying\u201d exercises with\n\nhim, in which he would pretend to be a \u201ccute\u201d child interested in engaging in sex acts with other\n\n\u201cCUTE!* :-P\u201d prepubescent children. [Id. at \u00b6 125]. In one instance, the defendant sent another\n\nuser 25 images of two prepubescent girls engaging in sex acts with each other along with the\n\nprompt \u201cus?,\u201d presumably to initiate a role-playing fantasy. [Id. at \u00b6 127(a)].\n\nWebsite A launched in approximately 2017. [PSR at \u00b6 6]. By June 2022, agents with the\n\nFBI had arrested its number-two administrator\u2014Selwyn Rosenstein\u2014who helped run the site\n\nfrom his home in Florida. [Id. at \u00b6 20]. Five months later, the FBI arrested the leader of the site\u2014\n\nWilliam Spearman\u2014and charged him with running this sprawling conspiracy. [Id. at \u00b6\u00b6 4, 44-66].\n\nWebsite A proved too unwieldy for the remaining administrators to manage alone, however, and\n\nit\u2014along with three other dark-web child pornography sites that Spearman helped run\u2014were\n\ndismantled as a result.\n\nAfter Website A ceased operating in its original form, the defendant migrated to other\n\nchild-pornography sites on the dark web, where he continued to traffic in images of minors being\n\nsexually abused, role-play the sexual abuse of children with other like-minded offenders, and\n\nperform moderating duties. [PSR at \u00b6\u00b6 128, 129, 130(f)]. Law enforcement, however, continued\n\nto investigate users of Website A. As a result of these investigative efforts, offenders who\n\ncontributed to Website A\u2019s criminal enterprise from around the United States and abroad\u2014\n\n4\nCase 9:24-cr-80053-AMC Document 98 Entered on FLSD Docket 07/11/2025 Page 5 of 17\n\nincluding in the United Kingdom, the Netherlands, Italy, Germany, Estonia, Belgium, and South\n\nAfrica\u2014were identified and apprehended. This defendant\u2019s criminal conduct was put to an end in\n\nJune 2024, when the FBI executed a warrant to search his home in Minnesota and arrested him.\n\n[Id. at \u00b6 130]. After his arrest, a federal grand jury in the Southern District of Florida returned a\n\nsuperseding indictment charging him with conspiracy to advertise and conspiracy to distribute\n\nchild pornography. [ECF No. 26]. In March 2025, this Court accepted the defendant\u2019s plea of\n\nguilty to both counts, [ECF No. 91], which was supported by a factual proffer, [ECF No. 88].\n\nSENTENCING ANALYSIS\n\n\u201cIn sentencing a defendant, first the district court \u2018shall consider ... the kinds of sentence\n\nand the sentencing range established for ... the applicable category of offense committed by the\n\napplicable category of defendant as set forth in the guidelines.\u2019\u201d 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 3553(a)(4)(A). The\n\nSentencing Guidelines are thus the appropriate starting point from which the Court exercises its\n\ndiscretion under \u00a7 3553(a). See United States v. Crawford, 407 F.3d 1174, 1178\u201379 (11th Cir.\n\n2005). This Court therefore \u201cmust first calculate the Guidelines range, and then consider what\n\nsentence is appropriate for the individual defendant in light of the statutory sentencing factors, 18\n\nU.S.C. \u00a7 3553(a), explaining any variance from the former with reference to the latter.\u201d Nelson v.\n\nUnited States, 129 S. Ct. 890, 891-92 (2009).\n\nI. THE APPLICABLE SENTENCING GUIDELINES\n\nThe U.S. Probation Office has filed a PSR with the following Guidelines calculation:\n\nGuideline Offense\nLevel\nBase Offense Level (U.S.S.G. \u00a7 2G2.2(a)(2)); 22\nEnhancement for trafficking in material that involved a prepubescent minor and\na minor who had not attained the age of 12 years (id. \u00a7 2G2.2(b)(2)); +2\nEnhancement for distributing in exchange for non-pecuniary consideration (id.\n\u00a7 2G2.2(b)(3)(B)); +5\nEnhancement for trafficking in material that portrays masochistic conduct or\n\n5\nCase 9:24-cr-80053-AMC Document 98 Entered on FLSD Docket 07/11/2025 Page 6 of 17\n\nother depictions of violence or sexual abuse of exploitation of an infant or +4\ntoddler (id. \u00a7 2G2.2(b)(4));\nEnhancement for use of a computer or interactive computer service for receipt\nof child pornography (id. \u00a7 2G2.2(b)(6)); +2\nEnhancement for an offense involving more than 600 images (id. \u00a7\n2G2.2(b)(7)(D)); +5\nEnhancement for being a manager/supervisor of criminal activity involving\nmore than five individuals (id. \u00a7 3B1.1(b)); +3\nReduction for demonstration of acceptance of responsibility (id. \u00a7 3E1.1(a)); -2\nReduction for timely notification of intent to plead guilty (id. \u00a7 3E1.1(b)). 3 -1\nTOTAL: 40\n\n[PSR at \u00b6\u00b6 148-63]. This calculation accurately reflects the facts underlying the defendant\u2019s\n\noffenses and his relevant conduct. The PSR further places him in Criminal History Category I,\n\nwhich results in a Guidelines sentencing range of 292 to 365 months in prison. [Id. at \u00b6 205].\n\nII. THE 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 3553(A) FACTORS WARRANT A 292-MONTH SENTENCE\n\nCareful consideration of the sentencing factors enumerated in \u00a7 3553(a) confirms that there\n\nis no reason to vary or depart from the Guidelines range here. In particular, \u00a7 3553(a)(1) provides\n\nthat courts must consider the nature and circumstances of the offense as well as the history and\n\ncharacteristics of the defendant in crafting a sentence. Additional factors outlined in \u00a7 3553(a)(2)\n\ninclude the need for the sentence to reflect the seriousness of the offense; promote respect for the\n\nlaw; provide just punishment; afford adequate deterrence to criminal conduct; protect the public\n\nfrom further crimes of the defendant; and provide the defendant with needed education or\n\nvocational training, medical care, or other corrective treatment in the most effective manner.\n\nSection 3553(a) also instructs courts to consider the need to avoid sentencing disparities between\n\nsimilarly situated defendants. See 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 3553(a)(6). Based on these factors, a 292-month\n\nsentence is sufficient but not greater than necessary to achieve the purposes set forth in \u00a7 3553(a).\n\n3\nIf the Court finds that the defendant has accepted responsibility and is entitled to a two-level reduction\nunder U.S.S.G. \u00a7 3E1.1(a), the United States moves to adjust his offense level downward by one level\npursuant to \u00a7 3E1.1(b) for his timely guilty plea.\n\n6\nCase 9:24-cr-80053-AMC Document 98 Entered on FLSD Docket 07/11/2025 Page 7 of 17\n\nA. The Nature and Circumstances of the Offenses\n\nThe PSR provides a thorough overview of Website A and the conduct of the defendant and\n\nhis co-conspirators. In short, Website A was a sprawling and complex criminal enterprise catering\n\nto pedophiles and enthusiasts of child pornography. Anyone who encountered it would have\n\nimmediately understood that it was dedicated to the exchange of images and videos depicting the\n\nrape, abuse, and exploitation of minors. Users of the website\u2014of which there were thousands\u2014\n\ntrafficked in large volumes of child pornography and discussed the sexual abuse of minors for\n\nyears. This high volume of activity was by design: the users who ran the site required other users\n\nto share child pornography in order to gain greater access to the site.\n\nBut Website A was more than just a website or a place to acquire images and videos of\n\nminors being sexually abused. It was a flourishing community where offenders could come\n\ntogether, discuss their interests, share information, provide advice on how to avoid law\n\nenforcement, and normalize one another\u2019s sexual desire for young children. Offenders who\n\ngravitate to these sorts of communities are more dangerous than other trafficking offenders\u2014like\n\nthose who obtain their child pornography through more impersonal methods of distribution\u2014\n\nbecause these online communities allow offenders to validate their sexual interest in children \u201cso\n\nthat actual sexual contact with a child no longer seems as wrong\u201d and the step from possession to\n\nproduction of child pornography no longer seems as drastic. United States v. Klear, 3 F. Supp. 3d\n\n1298, 1304 (M.D. Ala. 2014). And, to make matters worse, the technology underlying Website A\n\nwas designed to allow these offenders to engage in these activities anonymously but openly, and\n\nwith a measure of assurance that their crimes would not be detected. [PSR \u00b6 11].\n\nAll of this was possible because of the dedicated work of the defendant and the other\n\nmoderators of Website A, who facilitated child exploitation offenses on a massive, global scale.\n\n7\nCase 9:24-cr-80053-AMC Document 98 Entered on FLSD Docket 07/11/2025 Page 8 of 17\n\nThe defendant was therefore not a casual, tepid, or conflicted participant in this criminal enterprise.\n\nInstead, he fully committed himself to Website A, rose through the ranks to become one of a select\n\ngroup of members who oversaw the thousands of lower-level users on the site, and spent significant\n\namounts of his time chatting with other users, sharing child pornography on the site, and making\n\nsure others followed the rules. This was not a crime of opportunity or the result of a momentary\n\nlapse of judgment. To the contrary, defendant\u2019s crimes took sustained planning, effort, and time.\n\nThe conduct and the concomitant harm he caused was both real and significant.\n\nAlthough this Court must consider other factors beyond the defendant\u2019s offense conduct,\n\nthis Court should nonetheless give significant weight to his activity on Website A at sentencing.\n\nSee, e.g., United States v. Croteau, 819 F.3d 1293, 1309 (11th Cir. 2016) (\u201cThe weight given to\n\nany specific \u00a7 3553(a) factor is committed to the sound discretion of the district court.\u201d).\n\nB. The History and Characteristics of the Defendant\n\nThe defendant\u2019s history and characteristics also weigh strongly in favor of a 292-month\n\nsentence.\n\nWhile it is true that this is the defendant\u2019s first criminal conviction, it also true that he\n\nengaged in ceaseless criminal activity on Website A for months, if not longer. [PSR at \u00b6 124]. And\n\ngiven the level of familiarity with online-child-exploitation communities that one would need to\n\nfind a site like Website A\u2014not to mention the dedication it takes to get promoted to a moderator\n\non the site and the fact that the defendant migrated to different child-pornography sites after\n\nWebsite A ceased operating in its original form\u2014it stands to reason that the defendant\u2019s child-\n\npornography-related conduct extends far beyond the timeframe during which he was active on\n\nWebsite A. Indeed, the defendant told the FBI during the search of his home that he found Website\n\nA while perusing another dark-web site, that he visited several other similar sites, and that he was\n\n8\nCase 9:24-cr-80053-AMC Document 98 Entered on FLSD Docket 07/11/2025 Page 9 of 17\n\nfamiliar with encryption software and sometimes used that software when accessing the dark web,\n\nwhere he engaged in his illegal conduct. [Id. at 130]. In other words, he may have no previous\n\nconvictions, but that is only because he has never been caught. Being talented at committing crimes\n\nwithout being detected is not a mitigating factor.\n\nThe Court should also conclude that the defendant was motivated to commit these offenses\n\nby his sexual attraction to prepubescent children. He admitted to the FBI that he is attracted to\n\nunderage girls, and he regularly posted links on Website A depicting the sexual abuse and\n\nexploitation of prepubescent girls, including girls as young as eight years old. [Id. at \u00b6\u00b6 130(c)-(d),\n\n131]. The FBI also found images of child pornography on devices in his home, including content\n\ndepicting the sexual abuse of very young children. [Id. at \u00b6 130].\n\nThe only conceivable explanation for this conduct is that the defendant harbors a deep\n\nsexual interest in children. This is of particular concern given that there is reason to believe the\n\ndefendant is very tech-savvy and thus less likely to be caught in the future than the average child-\n\npornography offender. As outlined above, he is a sophisticated and likely experienced consumer\n\nof child pornography, which he parlayed into a leadership role on Website A. He understood the\n\nimportance of using the dark web to avoid detection. He also took steps to conceal his criminal\n\nconduct from those closest to him, including his wife and child. He is therefore far more capable\n\nthan the average offender of continuing to exploit children online without detection upon his\n\nrelease from incarceration.\n\nSeeking to rebut this conclusion, the defendant has provided the government with a psycho-\n\nsexual evaluation report prepared by Dr. William Samek, who has a Ph.D. in forensic and clinical\n\npsychology. [See PSR at \u00b6 182]. The government anticipates that the defense will submit this report\n\nas mitigating evidence for sentencing, but this Court should give it little weight. To start, the report\n\n9\nCase 9:24-cr-80053-AMC Document 98 Entered on FLSD Docket 07/11/2025 Page 10 of 17\n\nappears to be based almost entirely on information gleaned over the course of a single six-hour\n\ninterview with the defendant, meaning Dr. Samek reached his conclusions without reviewing any\n\nof the underlying evidence in this case, including any of the child pornography found on the\n\ndefendant\u2019s computer or the original posts he made on Website A. Nor does it appear that Dr.\n\nSamek administered any sort of diagnostic tests or examinations on the defendant before forming\n\nhis diagnostic impressions. In other words, to reach his conclusions, Dr. Samek relied entirely on\n\nthe charging documents and the information provided to him by the defendant, who has every\n\nreason to conceal from Dr. Samek any feelings, impulses, or past actions that would harm his\n\nprospects at sentencing.\n\nWhile the government harbors significant skepticism of the reliability of a psychologist\u2019s\n\ndiagnostic impressions that are based on a single interview with no diagnostic testing and\n\napparently no attempts to corroborate or disprove the interviewee\u2019s assertions, the government is\n\neven more concerned by Dr. Samek\u2019s conclusion that the defendant is \u201cextremely unlikely to\n\nrecidivate.\u201d [PSR at \u00b6 182]. The only support he cites for this conclusion are his own experience\n\nwith men like the defendant and unspecified research. But it stands to reason that Dr. Samek\u2019s\n\nprimary experience with the criminal justice system is preparing psycho-sexual evaluation reports\n\nfor offenders like the defendant to use at sentencing based on a single interview and no follow-up,\n\nwhich is hardly an adequate basis to divine whether this particular defendant is likely to recidivate.\n\nAnd while some psychologists administer certain tests\u2014the Static-99R and the CPORT, for\n\nexample\u2014when attempting to form an opinion on a defendant\u2019s risk of recidivism, see, e.g., Henry\n\nv. Abernathy, 647 F. Supp. 3d 12351242-43 (M.D. Ala. 2022) (discussing various \u201ctools that are\n\nused to assess an individual\u2019s risk of sexual recidivism\u201d), Dr. Samek appears not to have done so\n\nhere. That is likely because, to the extent these sorts of risk assessment tools have any predictive\n\n10\nCase 9:24-cr-80053-AMC Document 98 Entered on FLSD Docket 07/11/2025 Page 11 of 17\n\nvalue, that value completely evaporates in cases like this one, where the defendant is facing a term\n\nof incarceration of at least 15 years and his circumstances will be materially different upon his\n\nrelease from prison. Dr. Samek\u2019s untested hypothesis that the defendant presents little risk of\n\nreoffending at this moment in time is thus of no value to this Court\u2019s sentencing determination.\n\nC. The Need for the Sentence Imposed to Reflect the Seriousness of the Offense,\nPromote Respect for the Law, and Provide Just Punishment\n\nIt is beyond dispute that crimes involving the trafficking of child pornography are serious\n\nand warrant significant sanctions. As \u201c[l]ong-term studies\u201d have confirmed, \u201csexual abuse is\n\ngrossly intrusive in the lives of children and is harmful to their normal psychological, emotional\n\nand sexual development in ways which no just or humane society can tolerate.\u201d United States v.\n\nIrey, 612 F.3d 1160, 1207 (11th Cir. 2010) (en banc) (collecting authority). The fact that the\n\ndefendant\u2019s conduct of conviction does not involve him committing hands-on acts of child sexual\n\nabuse does not lessen the significance of his predatory and criminal behavior. Contrary to what\n\nsome may suggest, trafficking in images of child sexual abuse inherently involves the sexual abuse\n\nof children, even if no new contraband is produced. See New York v. Ferber, 458 U.S. 747, 759\n\n(1982). In fact, as explained above, the defendant\u2019s community-building activity on Website A\n\nhere very likely played a role in encouraging other users to escalate from possessing to producing\n\nchild pornography. Accordingly, far from being victimless, trafficking in images of child sexual\n\nabuse \u201charms children in part because it drives production [of child pornography], which involves\n\nchild abuse.\u201d Paroline v. United States, 572 U.S. 434, 439 (2014).\n\nTrafficking in child pornography also results in perpetual harm to children who have\n\nalready been sexually abused, all of whom are grappling with the indelible trauma caused by that\n\nabuse. See id. at 440. As another court noted, \u201c[i]t is well established that children featured in child\n\npornography are harmed by the continuing dissemination and possession of that pornography.\n\n11\nCase 9:24-cr-80053-AMC Document 98 Entered on FLSD Docket 07/11/2025 Page 12 of 17\n\nSuch images are \u2018a permanent record of the children\u2019s participation and the harm to the child is\n\nexacerbated by their circulation.\u2019\u201d United States v. Burgess, 684 F.3d 445, 459 (4th Cir. 2012)\n\n(quoting Ferber, 458 U.S. at 759). Accordingly, \u201c[e]very instance of viewing images of child\n\npornography represents a renewed violation of the privacy of the victims and a repetition of their\n\nabuse.\u201d Adam Walsh Child Protection and Safety Act of 2006, Pub. L. No. 109-248, \u00a7 501(2)(D),\n\n120 Stat. 587, 624 (2006) (codified at 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 2251 note); see also United States v. Sherman,\n\n268 F.3d 539, 547 (7th Cir. 2001) (recognizing that \u201c[t]he possession, receipt and shipping of child\n\npornography directly victimizes the children portrayed by violating their right to privacy, and in\n\nparticular violating their individual interest in avoiding the disclosure of personal matters\u201d). These\n\nchildren, \u201cwho must live with the knowledge that adults like [the defendant] can pull out a picture\n\nor watch a video that has recorded the abuse of [them] at any time,\u201d \u201csuffer a direct and primary\n\nemotional harm\u201d when offenders consume, receive, and advertise this child pornography.\n\nSherman, 268 F.3d at 547-48.\n\nIn light of the very serious harm caused by the defendant\u2019s conduct\u2014and the equally\n\nserious harm that would befall his victims if he were to recidivate\u2014it is particularly important that\n\nthis Court craft a sentence that provides just punishment. 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 3553(a)(2)(A). As the\n\nEleventh Circuit has observed, \u201cbecause punishment should fit the crime, the more serious the\n\ncriminal conduct is the greater the need for retribution and the longer the sentence should be. The\n\nseriousness of a crime varies directly with the harm it causes or threatens. It follows that the greater\n\nthe harm the more serious the crime, and the longer the sentence should be for the punishment to\n\nfit the crime.\u201d Irey, 612 F.3d at 1206. The Irey court cited the Senate Report regarding this\n\nprovision:\n\nThis purpose\u2014essentially the \u201cjust deserts\u201d concept\u2014should be reflected clearly\nin all sentences; it is another way of saying that the sentence should reflect the\n\n12\nCase 9:24-cr-80053-AMC Document 98 Entered on FLSD Docket 07/11/2025 Page 13 of 17\n\ngravity of the defendant\u2019s conduct. From the public\u2019s standpoint, the sentence\nshould be of a type and length that will adequately reflect, among other things, the\nharm done or threatened by the offense, and the public interest in preventing a\nrecurrence of the offense.\n\nId. (quoting S.Rep. No. 98-225, at 75-76, 1984 U.S.C.C.A.N. 3258-59).\n\nPut differently, punishment is the way in which society expresses denunciation of\n\nwrongdoing, and it is thus essential that this Court promote and maintain respect for the law by\n\npronouncing sentences that fully reflect society\u2019s revulsion toward the sexual abuse of children.\n\nSee Gregg v. Georgia, 428 U.S. 153, 184 n.30 (1976) (citing Royal Commission on Capital\n\nPunishment, Minutes of Evidence, Dec. 1, 1949, p. 207 (1950)). In this sense, retribution is a\n\nparticularly valid penological goal in a case like this one, where the defendant\u2019s crimes caused\n\nunfathomable harm to our society\u2019s most vulnerable victims.\n\nThis Court must also consider the need to \u201cpromote respect for the law\u201d at sentencing. 18\n\nU.S.C. \u00a7 3353(a)(2)(A). Congress enacted federal criminal laws targeting child-pornography\n\noffenses to address the ongoing sexual abuse and victimization of children. S. Rep. 95-438, 5, 1978\n\nU.S.C.C.A.N. 40, 42-43, 56. But this legislation and its later amendments are more than\n\nprophylactic measures. They reflect value judgments and the accepted moral norms of our society.\n\nAs one Senate Judiciary Committee report concluded: \u201cthe use of children\u2026as the subjects of\n\npornographic materials is very harmful to both the children and the society as a whole,\u201d describing\n\nthe conduct as \u201coutrageous.\u201d S. Rep. 95-438, 5, 1978 U.S.C.C.A.N. at 43.\n\nAgainst this backdrop, the defendant did not just violate federal law, he also transgressed\n\nuniversally recognized moral principles\u2014specifically, the need to protect and nurture children and\n\nnot to be titillated by depictions of their rape and abuse. Through his own conduct and the conduct\n\nthat he facilitated through his moderation and support of Website A, the defendant exploited\n\nthousands upon thousands of children and helped to revictimize them through the continued\n\n13\nCase 9:24-cr-80053-AMC Document 98 Entered on FLSD Docket 07/11/2025 Page 14 of 17\n\ntrafficking in material depicting their abuse. This Court should craft a sentence that appropriately\n\nexpresses social condemnation of the defendant\u2019s crimes, captures the serious harm he has caused,\n\nand promotes respect for the rule of law. The government\u2019s recommended sentence of 292 months\n\nof imprisonment does all three.\n\nD. The Need for the Sentence Imposed to Afford Adequate Deterrence and Protect\nthe Public\n\nBoth specific and general deterrence call for a significant sentence consistent with the\n\nGuidelines recommendation here. With respect to specific deterrence, the risk the defendant poses\n\nto children is immense. His crimes are easily explained by way of analogy: \u201cHow many people\n\nwho collect baseball cards have also played the game (or would play, if given the opportunity)? In\n\nother words, do the things we collect reflect our fantasies and interests? An answer in the\n\naffirmative seems obvious.\u201d Handbook of Behavioral Criminology, p. 332, edited by Dr. Vincent\n\nB. Van Hasselt and Dr. Michael L. Bourke, Springer Intl. Publishing (2017).\n\nHere, the defendant collected cards in the form of sexually explicit images and videos of\n\nchildren. But when viewing this material, he was not simply fantasizing about expanding the list\n\nof images and videos he had viewed. Common sense instructs that he was viewing this material\n\nbecause he very much desired the opportunity to engage in the abusive sexual conduct he observed.\n\nAnd as the Supreme Court has noted, there are \u201cgrave concerns over the high rate of recidivism\n\namong convicted sex offenders and their dangerousness as a class.\u201d Smith v. Doe, 538 U.S. 84,\n\n103 (2003). The defendant now fits squarely within that class. The sentence imposed must\n\ntherefore protect the public and account for the specific danger he poses to minors.\n\nMore generally, \u201cthe deterrence objective of sentencing is \u2018particularly compelling in the\n\nchild pornography context.\u2019\u201d Irey, 612 F.3d at 1211 (citation omitted). \u201c[I]mposing a lighter\n\nsentence on one convicted of a child pornography offense \u2018tends to undermine the purpose of\n\n14\nCase 9:24-cr-80053-AMC Document 98 Entered on FLSD Docket 07/11/2025 Page 15 of 17\n\ngeneral deterrence, and in turn, tends to increase (in some palpable if unmeasurable way) the child\n\npornography market.\u2019\u201d Id. (citation omitted). As the Eleventh Circuit noted in a similar case,\n\n\u201c[y]oung children were raped in order to enable the production of the pornography that the\n\ndefendant both downloaded and uploaded\u2014both consumed himself and disseminated to others.\u201d\n\nUnited States v. Pugh, 515 F.3d 1179, 1194 (11th Cir. 2008) (quoting United States v. Goldberg,\n\n491 F.3d 668, 672 (7th Cir. 2007)). The same is true here. And given that \u201csentences influence\n\nbehavior,\u201d it follows that \u201cthe lighter the punishment for downloading and uploading child\n\npornography, the greater the customer demand for it and so the more will be produced.\u201d Id. For\n\nthis reason, \u201cgeneral deterrence is crucial in the child pornography context.\u201d United States v.\n\nBistline, 665 F.3d 758, 767 (6th Cir. 2012) (quoting United States v. Camiscone, 591 F.3d 823,\n\n834 (6th Cir. 2010)) (punctuation altered). In recognition of the importance of deterring both the\n\ndefendant and others from engaging in this sort of criminal conduct in the future, the United States\n\nsubmits that a sentence of 292 months is appropriate.\n\nE. The Need to Avoid Unwarranted Sentencing Disparities and the Kinds of Sentences\nAvailable\n\nThe United States\u2019 292-month recommendation is also consistent with the sentences this\n\nCourt has already handed down to the defendant\u2019s co-conspirators. To date, the Court has\n\nsentenced eight defendants for their involvement in the leadership of Website A:\n\nMatthew Branden Garrell Sentenced to 250 months in prison.\n\nRobert Preston Boyles Sentenced to 280 months in prison.\n\nJoseph Robert Stewart Sentenced to 285 months in prison.\n\nGregory Malcolm Good Sentenced to 310 months in prison.\n\nSelwyn David Rosenstein Sentenced to 336 months (28 years) in prison.\n\nJoseph Addison Martin Sentenced to 504 months (42 years) in prison.\n\n15\nCase 9:24-cr-80053-AMC Document 98 Entered on FLSD Docket 07/11/2025 Page 16 of 17\n\nKeith David McIntosh Sentenced to 660 months (55 years) in prison.\n\nWilliam Michael Spearman Sentenced to life in prison.\n\nThe defendant and his eight co-conspirators all held positions of leadership on Website A,\n\nand some held similar positions on other child-pornography sites on the dark web. In addition to\n\nadvertising and distributing child pornography online, these offenders used their positions of\n\nleadership on Website A and elsewhere to promulgate rules for these websites, enforce those rules\n\nby banning or scolding users who violated them, hold staff meetings, recruit members to serve as\n\nstaff members, recommend users for promotion, edit and delete user posts, praise individuals for\n\nparticipating in and contributing to Website A\u2019s criminal enterprise, keep records of child-\n\npornography posts made by individual members, pay for and maintain the website servers, and\n\nengage in other criminal matters.\n\nOf the above co-conspirators, this defendant\u2019s role on the site was most analogous to the\n\nroles that Garrell and Boyles played. Though not founding members of the site and not necessarily\n\namong the upper-most echelon of leaders on the site, all three were nevertheless promoted to\n\nmoderators based on their active contributions to Website A and therefore exercised a degree of\n\nauthority and control over the site\u2019s rank-and-file users. And unlike Garrell and Boyles, the\n\ndefendant here continues to minimize and down-play his involvement on the site. Indeed, despite\n\nadmitting to the FBI that he was a moderator and that he advertised images on the site depicting a\n\nprepubescent child being anally and vaginally penetrated, the defendant now maintains that he\n\nplayed no moderating role on Website A and did not share any sadomasochistic material on the\n\nsite. [ECF No. 94]. The defendant\u2019s refusal to grapple with the role he played on Website A raises\n\nconcerns about whether he appreciates the severity of his conduct and will take the steps needed\n\nto ensure that he does not reoffend upon his release from prison. Accordingly, a 292-month\n\n16\nCase 9:24-cr-80053-AMC Document 98 Entered on FLSD Docket 07/11/2025 Page 17 of 17\n\nsentence, which falls at the low end of his Guidelines sentencing range, is warranted here.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nFor the foregoing reasons, the United States respectfully requests that the Court sentence\n\nthe defendant to 292 months in prison, followed by a lifetime of supervised release.\n\nRespectfully submitted,\n\nSteven J. Grocki\nChief\nChild Exploitation and Obscenity Section\n\nBy: s/William G. Clayman\nWilliam G. Clayman\nKyle P. Reynolds\nTrial Attorneys\nU.S. Dept. of Justice, Criminal Division\nCourt ID # A5502958\n1301 New York Avenue, NW\nWashington, DC 20005\nPhone: (202) 514-0040\nEmail: william.clayman@usdoj.gov\n\nCERTIFICATE OF SERVICE\n\nI HEREBY CERTIFY that on July 11, 2025, I electronically filed the foregoing document\nwith the Clerk of the Court using CM/ECF. I also certify that the foregoing document is being\nserved this day on all counsel of record on the attached Service List in the manner specified.\n\ns/William G. Clayman\nWilliam G. Clayman\nTrial Attorney\nU.S. Department of Justice, Criminal Division\n\n17"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
